<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mumbai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bombay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mumbai, formerly Bombay, is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built on a group of islands on the west coast. Portugal ceded the islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1661 as part of the dowry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> made them its main western base. As capital of the Bombay Presidency it grew into a great port and industrial city, and it was officially renamed Mumbai in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mumbai.jpg" alt="Mumbai" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rutiknatekar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumbai_Bandra-Worli_Sea_Link.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mumbai lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on a narrow peninsula of Salsette Island bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, Thane Creek and Vasai Creek, at the mouth of the Ulhas River. It has a deep natural harbour. The city grew from an archipelago of seven islands: the Isle of Bombay, Parel, Mazagaon, Mahim, Colaba, Worli and Old Woman's Island, also called Little Colaba. Greater Mumbai covers 603 square kilometres in two districts, Mumbai City, the southern Island City, and Mumbai Suburban.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The islands were inhabited since the South Asian Stone Age. They became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> in the 3rd century BCE, followed by the Satavahana dynasty in the 2nd century BCE, and Ptolemy mentioned them in 150 CE. The Koli fishing community, whose goddess Mumbadevi is believed to have given the city its name, settled the region around the beginning of the Common Era. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and then the Gujarat Sultanate ruled them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The islands were ceded to the Portuguese in 1535 and in 1661 passed to the British Crown, which leased them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1668.</p><p>Under British rule Bombay became the capital of the Bombay Presidency and a major trading and administrative centre. The Hornby Vellard project, begun in 1782 and completed in 1845, reclaimed land between the seven islands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> and unified them. With new roads and railways and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> boom of the 19th century, it made the city a major seaport. The Brihanmumbai Municipal Corporation was established in 1888. The city played a major part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement. After 1947 it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>, and after the Samyukta Maharashtra movement the capital of Maharashtra in 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mumbai is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and the financial and commercial capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its population was 12,442,373 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, with more than 18 million in the metropolitan region. Its buildings from the British period, such as Victoria Terminus, are largely in the Gothic Revival style, while the Gateway of India is Indo-Saracenic and Marine Drive has many Art Deco buildings. The city is also the centre of Bollywood, the Hindi film industry, whose name blends Bombay and Hollywood.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mumbai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bombay</b>’s cotton mills and the Tata steelworks at Jamshedpur, opened in 1912, showed the growth of Indian industry, but overall incomes grew slowly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It set up a factory at Masulipatnam in 1611, gained rights at Surat from the Mughal Emperor Jahangir in 1612, built a factory at Madras in 1640 and leased <b>Bombay</b> island in 1668; after the Anglo-Mughal War Aurangzeb let it settle at Calcutta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Humayun attacked Gujarat in 1535 and briefly occupied it, during which <b>Bombay</b>, Bassein and Daman passed to the Portuguese.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Indian cinema, with Hindi films from <b>Mumbai</b> and large industries in Tamil, Telugu and other languages, produces more films than any other country, and a diaspora of tens of millions carries Indian culture and influence abroad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The pearls were sold through merchants to markets in <b>Bombay</b> and beyond, and the trade linked Sharjah to India, Persia and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sheikhdom-of-sharjah">Sheikhdom of Sharjah · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet Rajputana was also the homeland of the Marwari merchant communities, whose firms, such as those of the Birla and Bajaj families, had become leading industrial houses in Calcutta, <b>Bombay</b> and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
